{"parentUuid":"927e203c-03fb-453c-8ea7-6de7eca92c21","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"cdcc7d23-2660-49f5-aece-4c59588ccb35","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019rFUPStrHPjr6duSHhwg6h","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have comprehensive information. Here is the complete analysis:\n\n---\n\n## 1. `git repack` vs `git gc`\n\n**`git gc`** is a high-level orchestrator that runs multiple housekeeping operations: repacking, pruning loose objects, pruning reflogs, rerere, and more. When it repacks, by default it calls `git repack -d -l` and with `--auto` it may consolidate all packs into a single pack (using `-A`).\n\n**`git repack`** is a lower-level command focused solely on pack consolidation. Key options relevant to your use case:\n\n- **`--keep-pack=<pack-name>`** -- Excludes the named pack from repacking. Equivalent to having a `.keep` file on the pack. The pack name is without the leading directory path (e.g., `pack-abc123.pack`). Can be specified multiple times.\n\n- **Promisor packs are automatically handled separately.** From the docs: *\"Promisor packfiles are repacked separately: if there are packfiles that have an associated '.promisor' file, these packfiles will be repacked into another separate pack, and an empty '.promisor' file corresponding to the new separate pack will be written.\"* So `git repack` already partitions promisor and non-promisor packs into two distinct groups and never mixes them.\n\n- **`--geometric=<factor>`** -- Arranges packs so each successive pack contains at least `<factor>` times the number of objects as the next-largest pack. Only the smallest set of packs needed to restore the geometric progression is rolled up. This is incremental -- it avoids repacking large packs.\n\n- **`--filter=<filter-spec>` and `--filter-to=<dir>`** -- Removes objects matching the filter from the resulting pack and puts them into a separate packfile. The `--filter-to` option redirects the filtered-out pack to a separate directory (useful with the alternates mechanism).\n\n- **`-a` / `-A`** -- Repack everything into a single pack. `-a` makes unreachable objects in old packs loose; `-A` makes them unreachable loose objects with their modification time set to now (so they survive until pruned).\n\n- **`--exclude-promisor-objects`** -- Omits objects known to be in the promisor remote, operating only on locally created objects.\n\n**Key takeaway:** You can run `git repack` without triggering any of the extra work `git gc` does (reflog expiry, pruning, etc.). The promisor/non-promisor partition is enforced automatically.\n\n## 2. Multi-Pack-Index (MIDX)\n\nYes, MIDX helps significantly. It consolidates pack lookups without repacking.\n\n**What it does:** Creates a single index file that spans multiple packfiles, providing O(log N) object lookup across any number of packs instead of O(M * log(N/M)) when searching M separate packs.\n\n**Key properties:**\n- The individual `.idx` files remain intact -- you can delete the MIDX file, disable `core.multiPackIndex`, or downgrade with zero data loss.\n- `core.multiPackIndex` defaults to **true** in modern Git, so it's already active.\n- **Incremental MIDX** allows chaining multiple MIDX layers so appending new packs doesn't invalidate the existing index.\n\n**Commands:**\n- `git multi-pack-index write` -- Creates/updates the MIDX file.\n- `git multi-pack-index expire` -- Deletes packs no longer referenced by the MIDX.\n- `git multi-pack-index repack --batch-size=<size>` -- Selects small packs and rolls them into larger ones, updating the MIDX. This is exactly what incremental-repack uses internally.\n\n**For your case:** If filtered fetches create many small promisor packs, writing an MIDX makes object lookups fast without needing to touch the packs at all. This is the cheapest possible operation.\n\n## 3. `git maintenance run --task=incremental-repack`\n\nThis task performs an incremental repack using the MIDX. It is explicitly designed to **avoid full gc**.\n\n**Two-step process:**\n1. `git multi-pack-index expire` -- Deletes pack-files that are no longer referenced by the MIDX (i.e., packs that were already rolled up in a previous cycle).\n2. `git multi-pack-index repack` -- Selects several small packs and combines them into a bigger one, updates the MIDX to point to the new pack. The old small packs become unreferenced (cleaned up on the next `expire`).\n\n**Critical design decision:** The dynamic batch size is computed with large cloned repos in mind -- it deliberately avoids repacking the one \"big\" pack from clone time. It only touches small packs.\n\n**Auto-trigger:** `maintenance.incremental-repack.auto` (default: 10) controls how many pack-files not in the MIDX are needed before the task runs automatically. Set to 0 to disable, negative to always run.\n\n**Does NOT run full gc.** The incremental-repack task is a standalone task. When using `maintenance.strategy=incremental`, the `gc` task is not scheduled at all -- it runs prefetch + commit-graph hourly, loose-objects + incremental-repack daily, and pack-refs weekly.\n\n**There is also a `geometric` strategy** (newer, and the default for manual maintenance): performs geometric repacking of packfiles (`git repack --geometric=<factor>`) and keeps auxiliary data structures current. This is a full replacement for the `gc` strategy and is recommended for large repositories.\n\n## 4. `git pack-objects` for Selective Repacking\n\nYes, `git pack-objects` can be used for selective repacking, but it is a plumbing command.\n\n**Relevant options:**\n- **`--stdin-packs`** -- Instead of reading object IDs from stdin, reads pack names. All objects in the named packs are packed together. Packs prefixed with `^` are excluded (their objects are treated as \"already have\"). This is what geometric repacking uses internally.\n- **`--exclude-promisor-objects`** -- Omits objects known to be in the promisor remote.\n- **`--cruft`** -- Reads pack names from stdin (prefixed with `-` for packs being deleted), collects unreachable objects into a separate cruft pack.\n\n**Example of selective repacking with `--stdin-packs`:**\n```bash\n# Repack only packs pack-aaa.pack and pack-bbb.pack into a new pack,\n# excluding objects already in pack-ccc.pack:\necho \"pack-aaa.pack\" | echo \"pack-bbb.pack\" | echo \"^pack-ccc.pack\" | \\\n  git pack-objects --stdin-packs .git/objects/pack/pack\n```\n\nThis is low-level. In practice, `git repack --geometric` or `git multi-pack-index repack` are higher-level wrappers that orchestrate `pack-objects` calls for you.\n\n## 5. GC Targeting Only Promisor Packs\n\n**Built-in separation:** Git's repack already treats promisor and non-promisor packs as two distinct partitions. When `git repack` runs (including when invoked by `git gc`), promisor packs are repacked into their own separate pack, and non-promisor packs are repacked separately. The two groups are never mixed.\n\n**However, there is no flag to say \"only repack promisor packs and leave everything else untouched.\"** The partition works both ways -- you cannot tell `git gc` or `git repack -a` to skip the non-promisor side entirely.\n\n**Workarounds to achieve \"only touch promisor packs\":**\n\n1. **`.keep` files:** Place `.keep` files next to all non-promisor packs. `git repack -a -d` and `git gc` will skip `.keep`-marked packs. Then only promisor packs get consolidated.\n\n2. **`--keep-pack`:** Use `git repack -d --keep-pack=pack-xxx.pack` for each non-promisor pack. More explicit but equivalent to `.keep` files.\n\n3. **`gc.bigPackThreshold`:** Set this to a size just below your main pack. Packs larger than this threshold are kept during `git gc`, effectively protecting the user's main pack. Caveat: if the number of kept packs exceeds `gc.autoPackLimit`, the threshold is ignored and all packs are consolidated.\n\n4. **Manual with `pack-objects --stdin-packs`:** List only promisor packs as input, exclude non-promisor packs with `^` prefix. Fully surgical but you'd need to manage the MIDX/cleanup yourself.\n\n5. **Incremental-repack via MIDX:** The most practical approach -- `git multi-pack-index repack --batch-size=<size>` will select only small packs to consolidate. If the user's main pack is large, it stays untouched naturally.\n\n## 6. Configuration Options That Control GC During Fetch\n\n| Config | Default | Effect |\n|--------|---------|--------|\n| `gc.auto` | 6700 | Number of loose objects before `gc --auto` triggers. **Set to 0 to disable all auto-gc**, including pack consolidation. |\n| `gc.autoPackLimit` | 50 | Number of non-`.keep` packs before `gc --auto` consolidates them. Set to 0 to disable pack consolidation trigger. |\n| `gc.bigPackThreshold` | 0 (disabled) | Packs larger than this are kept during gc. Overridden if kept packs exceed `gc.autoPackLimit`. |\n| `maintenance.auto` | true | Controls whether commands like `fetch` run `git maintenance run --auto` at the end. Set to `false` to disable. |\n| `fetch.writeCommitGraph` | false | If true, writes a commit-graph after fetching. Separate from gc. |\n| `gc.writeCommitGraph` | true | If true, gc rewrites the commit-graph when it runs. |\n| `maintenance.incremental-repack.auto` | 10 | Number of packs not in the MIDX before incremental-repack auto-triggers. |\n| `maintenance.strategy` | (none, or `geometric` for manual) | Can be `incremental` (avoids gc task entirely) or `geometric`. |\n\n**Most targeted approach for your case:**\n```bash\ngit -c gc.auto=0 fetch ...\n# or\ngit fetch --no-auto-gc ...\n```\n\n## 7. `--no-auto-gc` as a Fetch Flag\n\n**Yes, `--no-auto-gc` exists as a direct `git fetch` flag.** It is documented in `fetch-options`:\n\n> `--[no-]auto-maintenance`, `--[no-]auto-gc`: Run `git maintenance run --auto` at the end to perform automatic repository maintenance if needed. `--[no-]auto-gc` is a synonym. This is enabled by default.\n\nSo both of these are valid:\n```bash\ngit fetch --no-auto-gc\ngit fetch --no-auto-maintenance\n```\n\nThey are synonyms. `--no-auto-maintenance` is the newer name; `--no-auto-gc` is kept for backward compatibility.\n\nThe alternative config-based approach (`-c gc.auto=0`) is broader -- it disables gc triggers globally for that command invocation, including any internal gc heuristics. The `--no-auto-gc` flag specifically prevents the `git maintenance run --auto` call at the end of fetch.\n\n---\n\n## Practical Recommendations for Your Filtered-Fetch Scenario\n\nGiven that you're doing filtered fetches that create promisor packs and want to avoid triggering full gc on the user's repo:\n\n1. **Suppress auto-gc on fetch:** Use `git fetch --no-auto-gc` (or `--no-auto-maintenance`). This is the simplest and most direct approach.\n\n2. **If pack accumulation becomes a problem:** Use `git multi-pack-index write` after fetches to keep lookups fast without repacking. This is cheap and non-destructive.\n\n3. **If you need to consolidate accumulated promisor packs:** Use `git multi-pack-index repack --batch-size=<size>` which only touches small packs and leaves the user's main pack untouched. Or use `git repack --geometric=2 -d` which achieves the same via the geometric progression (only rolling up small packs).\n\n4. **If you want background maintenance without gc:** Set `maintenance.strategy=incremental` which schedules incremental-repack instead of gc. But this changes the user's repo config, which you probably don't want.\n\n5. **Nuclear option for protecting user packs:** Place `.keep` files on non-promisor packs, then `git repack -a -d` will only consolidate promisor packs. Remove `.keep` files when done.\n\nSources:\n- [Git - git-repack Documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-repack)\n- [Git - git-gc Documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-gc)\n- [Git - git-pack-objects Documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-pack-objects)\n- [Git - git-maintenance Documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-maintenance)\n- [Git - multi-pack-index Documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/multi-pack-index)\n- [Git - git-multi-pack-index Documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-multi-pack-index)\n- [Git - fetch-options Documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/fetch-options)\n- [Git - partial-clone Documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/partial-clone)\n- [Multi-Pack-Index (MIDX) Design Notes](https://www.kernel.org/pub//software/scm/git/docs/technical/multi-pack-index.html)\n- [Partial Clone Design Notes](https://www.kernel.org/pub/software/scm/git/docs/technical/partial-clone.html)\n- [maintenance: add incremental-repack task (patchwork)](https://patchwork.kernel.org/project/git/patch/00f47c48484c16987592d8b39fbd70e2744e3d4a.1596147867.git.gitgitgadget@gmail.com/)\n- [maintenance: add incremental-repack auto condition](https://github.com/git/git/commit/e841a79a131d8ce491cf04d0ca3e24f139a10b82)\n- [maintenance: auto-size incremental-repack batch](https://github.com/git/git/commit/a13e3d0ec87c9d12b93700375fceacdfb2c6885f)\n- [repack: add --filter=\\<filter-spec\\> option (patchwork)](https://patchwork.kernel.org/project/git/patch/20230812000011.1227371-6-christian.couder@gmail.com/)"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a0cee464c31607f63 (use SendMessage with to: 'a0cee464c31607f63' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 41017\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 1080700</usage>"}]}]},"uuid":"98678480-fff2-423c-ac47-d389cbc6bf34","timestamp":"2026-04-24T13:03:52.726Z","toolUseResult":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to understand the options git provides for managing pack files in a way that avoids triggering full gc while still cleaning up accumulated packs. Specifically:\n\n1. What does `git repack` offer vs `git gc`? Can you repack only specific packs (e.g., promisor packs)?\n2. Does `git multi-pack-index` (MIDX) help here — can it consolidate pack lookups without repacking?\n3. What's `git maintenance run --task=incremental-repack` do exactly? Does it avoid full gc?\n4. Can `git pack-objects` b