{"parentUuid":"378e8e35-db2c-4241-83c1-6c068859733d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TC8TpCoQ4ft4MV5AXM6rpL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That's the right question. Suppressing gc indefinitely is not an option — we'd be polluting the user's `.git/objects/pack/` with an ever-growing set of promisor packs. Here are the viable options, each with different tradeoffs:\n\n## Option 1: `--no-auto-gc` on fetch + periodic lightweight consolidation\n\nGit has `--no-auto-gc` (alias `--no-auto-maintenance`) as a direct fetch flag. Use it to prevent the race, then consolidate promisor packs ourselves in a targeted way:\n\n```\ngit fetch --no-auto-gc --no-tags --filter=blob:none <url> <refspec>\n```\n\nAfter our go-git operations complete (cherry-pick done, refs updated), consolidate our small promisor packs using:\n\n```\ngit multi-pack-index repack --batch-size=<size>\n```\n\nThis uses MIDX incremental-repack, which only rolls up small packs (our promisor packs) and naturally skips the user's large clone pack. Git already partitions promisor packs from non-promisor packs during repack — they're never mixed. So this is safe.\n\n**Pros**: Eliminates the race. Pack consolidation only touches small packs. Never repacks the user's data.  \n**Cons**: Adds a post-sync maintenance step. Requires deciding when/how often to consolidate.\n\n## Option 2: Stop using go-git for tree operations after fetch\n\nThe root cause is go-git's stale pack file reference. Replace `FlattenTree` (go-git) with `git ls-tree -r` (CLI) in the cherry-pick path. Git CLI handles pack lifecycle correctly — it re-reads pack indices as needed.\n\n```go\n// Instead of:\n//   checkpoint.FlattenTree(repo, tipTree, \"\", mergedEntries)\n// Use:\n//   git ls-tree -r --format='%(objectmode) %(objecttype) %(objectname)\\t%(path)' <tree-hash>\n```\n\n**Pros**: Eliminates the entire class of go-git/pack race conditions. No need to manage gc timing at all.  \n**Cons**: Bigger code change. Parsing `ls-tree` output adds complexity. BuildTreeFromEntries still uses go-git (but only writes, not reads from packs).\n\n## Option 3: MIDX-only approach (no repack, no gc suppression)\n\nWrite a multi-pack-index after each fetch instead of preventing gc:\n\n```\ngit fetch --no-auto-gc ...     # prevent race\ngit multi-pack-index write     # fast, just creates an index spanning all packs\n```\n\nMIDX makes lookups fast across any number of packs without consolidating them. Let the user's own gc/maintenance handle pack consolidation on its own schedule.\n\n**Pros**: Cheapest operation (just writes an index file). Never touches pack files at all.  \n**Cons**: Pack files still accumulate on disk (many small files). Doesn't clean up — relies on the user's gc eventually running.\n\n## Option 4: Don't use filtered fetches for the sync path\n\nThe fetch in `fetchAndRebaseSessionsCommon` is specifically for syncing the `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch. The cherry-pick only reads trees (FlattenTree), not blobs. But the blobs on the checkpoint branch are small metadata files (JSON, transcripts). The bandwidth savings from `--filter=blob:none` may not justify the promisor pack accumulation.\n\nSet `NoFilter: true` on this specific fetch:\n\n```go\nremote.Fetch(ctx, remote.FetchOptions{\n    Remote:   fetchTarget,\n    RefSpecs: []string{refSpec},\n    NoTags:   true,\n    NoFilter: true,  // Regular pack, no promisor accumulation\n})\n```\n\n**Pros**: Simplest change. No promisor packs from sync path. No gc interaction at all.  \n**Cons**: Downloads blobs we don't need for the merge. Loses the bandwidth optimization for the sync fetch. Other fetch paths using filtered fetches still accumulate promisor packs.\n\n## My recommendation\n\n**Option 2 + Option 1 as defense-in-depth.** \n\nThe go-git/pack race is a latent bug that can manifest any time an external process (user's IDE, background maintenance, manual gc) repacks while we're r